    "q-37196": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37196",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates.",
      "worked_solution": "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =(\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
