    "q-95137": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95137",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1),$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates.",
      "worked_solution": "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
