    "q-165283": {
      "dom_id": "step-q165283",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Given that $v(r,\\theta )={r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂v}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates.",
      "worked_solution": "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂v}{∂x}=\\frac{∂v}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $v$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂r}=2r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }=-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =(2r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
