    "q-95168": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95168",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Given that $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates.",
      "worked_solution": "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂g}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z}={x}^{2}+2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z} & ={r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
