    "q-95174": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95174",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Given that $h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2}{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates.",
      "worked_solution": "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z}=8{x}^{2}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =8{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
